//! Template caching system.
//!
//! This module provides disk-based caching for remote templates with
//! TTL-based and content-based (ETag/git) invalidation strategies.

pub mod entry;
pub mod revalidation;
pub mod store;
pub mod validation;

pub use entry::{CacheEntry, CacheMetadata};
pub use revalidation::{needs_revalidation, CacheRevalidator, RevalidationResult};
pub use store::CacheStore;
pub use validation::{format_duration, parse_ttl, CacheValidator, ValidationResult};

/// Get the default cache directory.
pub fn default_cache_dir() -> std::path::PathBuf {
    dirs::cache_dir()
        .unwrap_or_else(|| std::path::PathBuf::from("."))
        .join("bivvy")
        .join("templates")
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn default_cache_dir_valid() {
        let path = default_cache_dir();
        assert!(path.ends_with("templates"));
    }
}

content.thinking:
Now I can see the issue clearly. The `store` method in `store.rs` writes content and metadata in two separate, non-atomic operations:

```rust
pub fn store(...) -> Result<CacheEntry> {
    self.ensure_dir()?;
    let content_path = self.content_path(source_id, template_name);
    fs::write(&content_path, content)?;           // Step 1: write content
    let entry = CacheEntry::new(...);
    self.save_metadata(&entry)?;                   // Step 2: write metadata
    Ok(entry)


}
```

And `save_metadata` does:
```rust
fn save_metadata(&self, entry: &CacheEntry) -> Result<()> {
    let meta_path = self.metadata_path(&entry.source_id, &entry.template_name);
    let json = serde_json::to_string_pretty(entry)?;
    fs::write(&meta_path, json)?;
    Ok(())
}
```

Both use direct `fs::write` calls without the atomic temp-file-then-rename pattern. This means concurrent processes writing to the same cache entry can end up with partial writes, where one process completes the content file while another overwrites the metadata, leaving them out of sync.

The state store actually implements atomic writes correctly, but the cache store doesn't. I also notice the `remove` method has a race condition between checking if a file exists and actually deleting it. The fix needs to apply atomic writes to both content and metadata operations, and ensure they stay consistent.

